    "abstract": "Climate models project that the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) will weaken in the 21st century, but the magnitude is highly uncertain. Some of this uncertainty is structural, as most climate models neglect increasing meltwater from the Greenland ice sheet and do not explicitly capture mesoscale ocean eddies. Here, we quantify the impact of Greenland meltwater on the AMOC until 2100 under SSP5-8.5 forcing for the first time in a strongly eddying (1/10\u00b0 horizontal resolution) ocean model. The meltwater-induced additional AMOC weakening is small (0.6 $\\pm$ 0.2 Sv) compared to the weakening due to warming alone, and similar at high and low resolution. The same meltwater would cause a stronger AMOC weakening under present-day climate conditions. We link both resolution-independence and state-dependence to large-scale controls of the AMOC. Our results demonstrate that the background ocean state is more important than resolution in determining how Greenland meltwater affects the AMOC.",
    "field": "Physical oceanography",
    "problem": "Existing climate models often neglect increasing Greenland meltwater and use coarse resolutions that cannot capture ocean eddies, creating uncertainty about the meltwater's impact on the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) under future warming.",
    "method": "This study uses a high-resolution, strongly eddying ocean model to quantify the impact of Greenland meltwater on the AMOC by comparing simulations under a high-emission scenario with and without meltwater forcing.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify the impact of increasing Greenland meltwater on the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) during the 21st century using a high-resolution, strongly eddying ocean model under a strong climate change scenario.",
